No, thanks so much.

531
00:19:25.240 --> 00:19:26.540
You've ruined the magic of the show.

532
00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:27.900
Not to me, no.

533
00:19:29.080 --> 00:19:29.980
No, it's true.

534
00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.720
I feel like we're always trying to get a palm tree out of the back of a shot.

535
00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:34.120
Right.

536
00:19:34.580 --> 00:19:34.980
Yeah.

537
00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:36.860
Wish we could shoot in real Toledo.

538
00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:38.200
Maybe one day we'll get to go there.

539
00:19:38.600 --> 00:19:39.820
Well, don't count on it.

540
00:19:39.900 --> 00:19:41.040
We never went to Scranton.

541
00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:42.640
You guys never went, right?

542
00:19:43.100 --> 00:19:45.540
But they went and shot some B-roll, I think.

543
00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:46.720
I think they did.

544
00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:50.380
I think John shot like B-roll for the titles and everyone.

545
00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:51.440
Maybe they went once.

546
00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:54.140
Now I feel like we're like the two old jaded aunties.

547
00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:55.500
We're like, don't count.

548
00:19:56.720 --> 00:19:57.840
You're never going to.

549
00:19:57.840 --> 00:19:59.060
Don't hold your breath.

550
00:19:59.980 --> 00:20:01.840
Nine years we didn't go to Scranton.

551
00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:03.060
You're stuck in Glendale.

552
00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:04.480
That's as far as you'll go.

553
00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:10.660
Well, now Ned is going to greet Marv and Anne's husband, his brother, Lou.

554
00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:13.820
And then Esmeralda makes her entrance.

555
00:20:14.160 --> 00:20:14.800
Oh, yeah.

556
00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:18.360
And I think we need to discuss her gorgeous red gown.

557
00:20:18.700 --> 00:20:21.720
We did get a fan question from Regina C.

558
00:20:21.940 --> 00:20:23.720
in Barcelona, who said,

559
00:20:23.840 --> 00:20:24.360
Hi, ladies.

560
00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:28.620
When I saw this episode and Esmeralda appeared on screen with that stunning red dress,

561
00:20:28.620 --> 00:20:30.800
I knew I had to find out more about it.

562
00:20:30.940 --> 00:20:31.920
It's perfection.

563
00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:35.480
The color, the drama with the flowers and the puffy sleeves, the headpiece.

564
00:20:35.820 --> 00:20:36.860
Did they make it?

565
00:20:36.980 --> 00:20:37.680
Did they buy it?

566
00:20:37.880 --> 00:20:38.640
What are the details?

567
00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:41.940
So, Chelsea, I've been very obsessed with your wardrobe.

568
00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:42.960
Oh, thank you so much.

569
00:20:43.020 --> 00:20:43.700
This is true.

570
00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:45.360
And I have been going on these sites.

571
00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:47.560
One is called ShopYourTV.com.

572
00:20:47.720 --> 00:20:48.120
Oh, yeah.

573
00:20:48.500 --> 00:20:50.940
I'll sometimes go on that because I love mare sweaters.

574
00:20:51.060 --> 00:20:53.560
And I'll forget where they're from from the first season.

575
00:20:53.720 --> 00:20:57.200
So, I'll go to, like, buy them and try and find them on sale, like, a year later.

576
00:20:57.320 --> 00:20:57.660
Oh, yeah.

577
00:20:57.820 --> 00:20:59.580
So, one of yours was on Farfetch.

578
00:21:00.060 --> 00:21:01.060
Oh, stop it.

579
00:21:01.100 --> 00:21:02.620
That big block sweater.

580
00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:02.940
Yes.

581
00:21:02.940 --> 00:21:04.460
The black collar sweater with the collar.

582
00:21:04.740 --> 00:21:04.960
Yeah.

583
00:21:05.140 --> 00:21:05.320
Yeah.

584
00:21:05.780 --> 00:21:07.320
I have the details for you.

585
00:21:11.940 --> 00:21:15.620
It's off the shoulder, and it is also by Farfetch.

586
00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:19.520
So, I think Kathleen likes Farfetch, your costume designer.

587
00:21:19.520 --> 00:21:20.100
She sure does.

588
00:21:20.420 --> 00:21:24.100
So, according, though, to the website, when you purchase this dress, it does not have

589
00:21:24.100 --> 00:21:24.840
the big flowers.

590
00:21:25.120 --> 00:21:30.880
So, I traded emails with Kathleen Felix Hager, amazing costume designer, as we have said.

591
00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:31.160
Yeah.

592
00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:36.220
She sent over pictures, you guys, of the fitting she did with Sabrina, and the costume

593
00:21:36.220 --> 00:21:38.860
department did, in fact, add all of the big flowers.

594
00:21:39.040 --> 00:21:39.220
Yeah.

595
00:21:39.220 --> 00:21:40.660
She's incredible.

596
00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:41.660
She's amazing.

597
00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:43.780
I adore her, and she's a genius.

598
00:21:44.160 --> 00:21:47.140
She does so many other shows, and we're so lucky to have her.

599
00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:53.120
But I remember in the script, what made me laugh so hard during the table read, it described

600
00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:56.300
the dress Esmeralda walks in as Met Gala.

601
00:21:56.620 --> 00:22:00.000
Like, basically, she thinks she's at the Met Gala, but it's the Ohio Journalism Awards.

602
00:22:00.260 --> 00:22:02.720
And I was like, that is Esmeralda.

603
00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:07.300
And, like, of course, of course, if she isn't, and, like, of course she's going to wear a tiara.

604
00:22:07.300 --> 00:22:09.800
Like, there's no way she's not wearing a tiara.

605
00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:10.320
She has to.

606
00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:10.680
Right.

607
00:22:10.920 --> 00:22:14.260
But, yeah, I remember when she walked in that first day, we were dying laughing.

608
00:22:14.380 --> 00:22:18.140
It was, like, more ridiculous than you could ever even imagine.

609
00:22:18.260 --> 00:22:21.380
And she just, like, looked amazing in it, which was, like, it was just so fun.

610
00:22:21.580 --> 00:22:22.800
But, yeah, that dress was amazing.

611
00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:27.260
Well, next up, it looks like Ned is holding a rabbit's foot.

612
00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:28.780
People comment on it.

613
00:22:29.080 --> 00:22:32.640
He says it's not a rabbit's foot because he doesn't want to win.

614
00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:34.740
You know, I don't need to win.

615
00:22:35.060 --> 00:22:36.440
He's just happy to be there.

616
00:22:36.640 --> 00:22:39.820
He actually says it's part of a dead mouse that he found in the street.

617
00:22:40.040 --> 00:22:47.320
I feel like this is classic Ned because sometimes the thing that's the truth, he's like, I'll make up something way worse than what is the actual truth.

618
00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:47.840
Yes.

619
00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:50.620
That is such a great way to put Ned.

620
00:22:51.100 --> 00:22:58.520
And, honestly, it's, like, reminding me of what we've been shooting because I'm like, yes, that is an absolute fundamental character trait of his.

621
00:22:58.520 --> 00:23:07.320
And it's, like, so sweet and charming and also just, like, oh, my God, how could he possibly think that sounds better than what he's actually doing?

622
00:23:07.740 --> 00:23:10.260
But, yeah, that rabbit's foot made me laugh so hard.

623
00:23:10.460 --> 00:23:12.020
Do you have any good luck charms?

624
00:23:12.180 --> 00:23:16.220
Are you a good luck charm person or good luck rituals or anything like that?

625
00:23:16.360 --> 00:23:18.440
That's such a good question.

626
00:23:19.120 --> 00:23:20.160
I'm trying to think.

627
00:23:20.300 --> 00:23:24.840
I used to have a turtleneck I wore to, like, every audition that I've actually loSt.

628
00:23:24.840 --> 00:23:25.680
I'm trying to remember.

629
00:23:25.780 --> 00:23:27.600
I guess I didn't wear it to my paper audition.

630
00:23:27.600 --> 00:23:34.480
But I used to, like, I had, like, one thing that I was always like, okay, if I wear this, I'm, you know, I think I'll do a good job.

631
00:23:34.520 --> 00:23:35.560
And I, like, booked a couple of roles.

632
00:23:35.660 --> 00:23:40.940
And it was, like, a really shitty, like, old turtleneck I would wear to everything.

633
00:23:41.060 --> 00:23:45.700
I'm picturing you, like, auditioning for, like, okay, this movie takes place on a cruise in the Caribbean.

634
00:23:46.180 --> 00:23:49.000
I'm like, well, I have to wear my turtleneck or else I won't get it.

635
00:23:49.140 --> 00:23:50.880
I have to wear my turtleneck sweater.

636
00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:53.260
I think that, I'm not kidding.

637
00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:57.040
I think once or twice my agents were like, could you resubmit that and, like, wear something else?

638
00:23:57.040 --> 00:23:58.360
Because it's so wrong for the part.

639
00:23:58.580 --> 00:23:59.760
But it was my good luck charm.

640
00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:02.300
Besides that, not really.

641
00:24:02.680 --> 00:24:06.980
I mean, I have my, like, silly little, like, routines in the morning that sometimes I'm like, if I don't do this, I'll.

642
00:24:07.320 --> 00:24:09.600
But nothing, nothing like a good luck charm.

643
00:24:09.660 --> 00:24:10.100
Do you guys?

644
00:24:10.520 --> 00:24:11.980
I had a red blouse.

645
00:24:11.980 --> 00:24:20.620
Like, now that you mention this thing about auditioning and the same thing, I have this red blouse that if I wore it, I seemed to get the part.

646
00:24:20.900 --> 00:24:23.360
And so I wore it to every audition.

647
00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:25.140
Did you wear it to audition for Pam?

648
00:24:25.280 --> 00:24:27.280
No, because it wasn't appropriate for Pam.

649
00:24:27.420 --> 00:24:27.540
Okay.

650
00:24:27.840 --> 00:24:32.080
This was, like, when I had to play, like, a pediatric nurse, but sexy.

651
00:24:32.440 --> 00:24:34.720
Or, like, a school teacher, but hot.

652
00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:37.620
That's what this blouse was.

653
00:24:37.900 --> 00:24:38.780
Yes, of course.

654
00:24:38.780 --> 00:24:39.720
That's so funny.

655
00:24:40.120 --> 00:24:45.060
I don't really have a good, like, charm, but I do have one thing I do every time I fly.

656
00:24:45.240 --> 00:24:45.580
What?

657
00:24:46.160 --> 00:24:47.460
And now my kids do it.

658
00:24:47.520 --> 00:24:48.020
Is that bad?

659
00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:49.180
No, it's good.

660
00:24:49.280 --> 00:24:55.680
Okay, as I walk up to the plane, like, and I'm about to step through the threshold to get on it, I pat the plane.

661
00:24:55.860 --> 00:24:58.020
Like, come on, girl, you've got this.

662
00:24:59.500 --> 00:25:02.740
And I've watched a steward clock me doing it.

663
00:25:02.920 --> 00:25:05.160
Like, I, like, it's my turn to step.

664
00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:07.040
And I'm like, wait a second, pat, pat, pat, pat, pat.

665
00:25:07.040 --> 00:25:08.820
So, it's so sweet.

666
00:25:09.440 --> 00:25:10.420
Yeah, that's what I do.

667
00:25:10.500 --> 00:25:11.180
The planes are tired.

668
00:25:11.420 --> 00:25:12.760
They just came from somewhere else.

669
00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:13.660
Sometimes they need a little.

670
00:25:13.840 --> 00:25:14.360
I just got this.

671
00:25:14.360 --> 00:25:14.760
You got this.

672
00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:15.660
I'm a really old one.

673
00:25:15.780 --> 00:25:16.700
I'm like, she tie-tie.

674
00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:18.360
Those old ones.

675
00:25:18.440 --> 00:25:19.540
I'm like, what are we?

676
00:25:19.660 --> 00:25:20.640
What's happening here?

677
00:25:20.680 --> 00:25:20.960
Yeah.

678
00:25:21.180 --> 00:25:22.160
You get an extra pat.

679
00:25:26.040 --> 00:25:27.740
Okay, now we're in the opening credits.

680
00:25:28.340 --> 00:25:29.920
There's this runner Jenna and I loved.

681
00:25:30.080 --> 00:25:33.040
It's Adam and his wife, and they're living it up at the awards.

682
00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:39.520
And they're drinking, it cuts to her, and she's at this long sort of dessert table, and she's

683
00:25:39.520 --> 00:25:41.060
just, like, got her plate piled.

684
00:25:41.140 --> 00:25:45.000
She's, like, stuffing a brownie into her mouth, and she's adorable.

685
00:25:45.360 --> 00:25:50.060
And it just made me think, Jenna, so much of, like, when we would go to award shows and

686
00:25:50.060 --> 00:25:51.240
we would hoard food.

687
00:25:52.260 --> 00:25:54.340
Well, because there isn't a lot of food.

688
00:25:54.460 --> 00:25:55.320
This is what I hear.

689
00:25:55.460 --> 00:26:00.340
You know when it's, like, the little thin, like, cracker, but it's super skinny?

690
00:26:00.340 --> 00:26:01.120
What do you call that one?

691
00:26:01.180 --> 00:26:03.040
It looks like a straw, but it's a cracker.

692
00:26:03.220 --> 00:26:04.220
I don't even know.

693
00:26:04.420 --> 00:26:05.960
Like, not a breadstick.

694
00:26:06.180 --> 00:26:07.220
But it looks like two chopsticks.

695
00:26:07.400 --> 00:26:08.360
Sure, sure, sure, sure, sure.

696
00:26:08.460 --> 00:26:09.440
Two bread chopsticks.

697
00:26:09.440 --> 00:26:10.240
That's your bread.

698
00:26:10.460 --> 00:26:12.400
And then you get, like, a giant vat of wine.

699
00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:14.280
A giant thing of wine.

700
00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:20.700
But then also, I feel like there would always be this sort of, like, congealed, like, a

701
00:26:20.700 --> 00:26:22.440
shrimp pate or something.

702
00:26:22.640 --> 00:26:26.800
Like, something that feels like it had been on the table a while, so you're definitely

703
00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:27.960
not touching that.

704
00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:31.120
With, like, one lightly sliced carrot.

705
00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:32.320
Sure, sure.

706
00:26:32.720 --> 00:26:33.680
Of course, your salad.

707
00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:36.120
But then all the wine you could ever want.

708
00:26:36.380 --> 00:26:36.740
Fun.

709
00:26:36.940 --> 00:26:37.320
Fun.

710
00:26:37.820 --> 00:26:40.520
But we have hoarded, like, bread before.

711
00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:44.280
Would you guys, like, go out after and eat or was it before while you were getting ready?

712
00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:44.480
You had to.

713
00:26:44.680 --> 00:26:45.100
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

714
00:26:45.180 --> 00:26:45.800
You had to.

715
00:26:45.940 --> 00:26:51.400
Or, you know, the after parties would have food, but, like, do you make it over there?

716
00:26:51.400 --> 00:26:55.660
Because it's like, you know, I feel like you're a bride at a wedding.

717
00:26:55.960 --> 00:26:59.640
You're talking to everybody and you never get a water or any food at all.

718
00:26:59.900 --> 00:27:00.060
Yeah.

719
00:27:00.360 --> 00:27:00.680
Totally.

720
00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:02.040
This is kind of a tangent.

721
00:27:02.300 --> 00:27:02.940
Huge tangent.

722
00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:05.000
I'm sure you guys have talked about this on the pod.

723
00:27:05.140 --> 00:27:05.540
So sorry.

724
00:27:05.900 --> 00:27:07.720
When did you guys become, like, best friends?

725
00:27:09.040 --> 00:27:10.420
Your face is so cute.

726
00:27:10.620 --> 00:27:12.380
She's doing that face like, I'm going to cry.

727
00:27:12.540 --> 00:27:14.460
I'm going to cry before you even know the story.

728
00:27:14.460 --> 00:27:16.380
I mean, we always say it was basketball.

729
00:27:16.800 --> 00:27:17.200
Really?

730
00:27:17.200 --> 00:27:21.920
Yeah, I think the basketball episode, because we were mostly background in that.

731
00:27:22.100 --> 00:27:25.580
And so they just sat us on a bench for three days, 12 hours a day.

732
00:27:25.880 --> 00:27:28.080
And we just told each other our life stories.

733
00:27:29.240 --> 00:27:30.280
Okay, that was it.

734
00:27:30.360 --> 00:27:31.180
That's all I wanted to know.

735
00:27:31.320 --> 00:27:31.560
Sorry.

736
00:27:31.780 --> 00:27:32.680
So it was kind of there.

737
00:27:32.980 --> 00:27:33.200
Yeah.

738
00:27:33.400 --> 00:27:37.780
And then it was, like, nice that we just shared a little partition, you know?

739
00:27:37.920 --> 00:27:40.540
Like, we were close to each other in the bullpen.

740
00:27:40.640 --> 00:27:43.380
So I would just hop up and I'd pop my head over and she'd do the same.

741
00:27:43.520 --> 00:27:44.140
And we'd chat there.

742
00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:44.340
So sweet.

743
00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:48.820
I think it's why the writers actually eventually wrote a scene with our two characters at the

744
00:27:48.820 --> 00:27:49.660
partition talking.

745
00:27:49.880 --> 00:27:50.060
Yeah.

746
00:27:50.280 --> 00:27:51.540
Because they would see us doing it.

747
00:27:51.720 --> 00:27:51.980
Yeah.

748
00:27:52.200 --> 00:27:53.300
When Dwight froze sprinkles.

749
00:27:53.500 --> 00:27:54.280
I remember.

750
00:27:54.620 --> 00:27:57.620
But then we started going through similar things in life, you know?

751
00:27:57.740 --> 00:27:59.820
Like, when the show started, we didn't have kids.

752
00:27:59.820 --> 00:28:01.460
And then we had kids on the show.

753
00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:05.960
And then, you know, so it was, like, there were a lot of parts of our lives where, like,

754
00:28:05.960 --> 00:28:07.400
you needed a friend who understood.

755
00:28:07.660 --> 00:28:10.860
And similarly, like, just all the fame and stuff, you know?

756
00:28:10.860 --> 00:28:13.920
Like, I didn't have any famous friends.

757
00:28:13.940 --> 00:28:17.580
So I didn't know who to ask about any of this stuff.

758
00:28:18.040 --> 00:28:19.900
And we just figured it out together.

759
00:28:19.900 --> 00:28:21.180
And it was really helpful.

760
00:28:21.440 --> 00:28:24.040
It was helpful to admit that we didn't know things.

761
00:28:24.200 --> 00:28:24.440
Yeah.

762
00:28:24.740 --> 00:28:24.880
Yeah.

763
00:28:25.080 --> 00:28:25.420
You know?

764
00:28:25.600 --> 00:28:27.920
And then also, like, what'd you find out?

765
00:28:27.960 --> 00:28:28.320
What did you?

766
00:28:28.400 --> 00:28:28.820
How did you?

767
00:28:28.920 --> 00:28:29.160
Yeah.

768
00:28:29.380 --> 00:28:29.880
What is it?

769
00:28:29.980 --> 00:28:31.400
And then our families, too.

770
00:28:31.500 --> 00:28:32.260
Both of our families.

771
00:28:32.460 --> 00:28:35.420
Like, no one in our families is in show business.

772
00:28:35.720 --> 00:28:35.980
Same.

773
00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:36.320
Same.

774
00:28:36.320 --> 00:28:36.560
Right.

775
00:28:36.700 --> 00:28:41.360
So I was just like, where do you get the clothes?

776
00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:42.000
Yes.

777
00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:42.700
Where do the—

778
00:28:42.700 --> 00:28:44.140
Literally, where do you get the clothes?

779
00:28:44.200 --> 00:28:44.380
Yes.

780
00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:46.260
Because, like, we spent a lot of time on that.

781
00:28:46.380 --> 00:28:46.480
Yeah.

